WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.850
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:03.270
This is Stephanie Wong,
Cloud Developer Advocate.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:06.990
We are back with our own
Google fellow Eric Brewer.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.360
Last time, we talked
about his last 10 years

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.390
of building infrastructure
at Google and Kubernetes'

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:13.860
influence in the space.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.690
He mentioned how
he is now focusing

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.750
on creating processes for more
secure software supply chains.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.930
So Eric, how are
you doing today?

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.070
ERIC BREWER: Fantastic.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.348
It's good to see you again.

00:00:24.348 --> 00:00:25.890
STEPHANIE WONG: Good
to see you, too.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.110
All right, so let's
dive right into it.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.560
Last time, we were talking about
supply chains for software.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.500
Now, you were talking a little
bit about the risk space

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.280
and companies tied to it.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.290
So how is the supply chain risk
assessment happening today?

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.693
Why is the risk so large?

00:00:41.693 --> 00:00:43.110
ERIC BREWER: Well,
I think there's

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.840
a lot we need to do
here as an industry,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.030
and it's beyond the
power of just Google.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.870
In fact, it's really quite a
challenge for the whole planet.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.770
But I would say the first thing
we need to decide is which

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.670
projects we want to actually
bet on, because in some sense,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.968
any project you're
betting on for security,

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:04.260
you'd like to make sure that
there are people that care

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.140
about that project and are
paying attention to it and will

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.050
fix security vulnerabilities
as they arise,

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.420
because in practice, 99%
of our vulnerabilities--

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.920
literally 99%--

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.630
are not in the code that you
write for your application.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.150
They're in this very deep tree
of dependencies, some of which

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.650
you may know about, some
which you may not know about.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.300
And so the first thing is what
are those dependencies, which

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.310
ones are well-maintained,
which ones are not.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.230
We're starting to present
that kind of information.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.350
But to take it up a
level, it's actually

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.090
going to be important
for all industries

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.330
to try to understand
what pieces of software

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.190
do we want to bet on and why.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.930
And what do we do about the ones
that are not quite up to par

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:44.430
yet?

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:45.305
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:47.460
And whether some
companies know it

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.000
or whether they have come
to an acceptance of it,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.810
Kubernetes has become the de
facto way to build and deploy

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.540
many, many applications today.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.420
Or at least that's the North
Star for many organizations.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.350
And now it has several hundred
software library dependencies,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:04.860
right?

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.830
So what are we at Google
Cloud doing to help

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.917
reduce those vulnerabilities?

00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:11.250
ERIC BREWER: Well, there's tons.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.100
There's of course, CNCF,
we started as a home

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.570
for Kubernetes.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.510
And it's part of the Linux
Foundation, which we also are

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.580
big fans of and funders of.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.670
I personally helped create
Open SSF, Open Source Security

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:24.660
Foundation.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.970
That, in my mind,
started in 2019,

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.370
although I think it actually
launched in early 2020.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.430
But that foundation is, in
fact, explicitly targeted

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.740
at open source security
and the supply chain.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.360
It was created for that purpose.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.210
And fortunately, it was created
before this stuff really

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.148
became so visibly bad.

00:02:41.148 --> 00:02:42.690
But I could see it
coming, and that's

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.460
why we needed to
kind of start early.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.840
The other thing I would say is
that Google has a long history

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.340
of committing to open source.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.430
And in our recent response
to the White House,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.820
we committed another $100
million to open source

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.180
in the coming years.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:04.260
And it's important to set that
precedent because the repairs

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.210
we need-- there's a
lot of mundane work

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.420
to do to make open source as
secure as we need it to be.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.680
And it's not fair to say oh,
volunteers and others, please

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.040
work harder.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.400
That's not going to be
the sustainable path.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.560
And in fact, I'm arguing
for federal funding

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.840
as well for open
source security.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.560
I kind of feel like we need
to get to a sustainable model

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.940
where the ongoing work
of security, which is not

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.270
easy stuff, is actually
financially supported,

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.667
not just for this year when it's
a crisis, but for the future.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.250
By the way, I think
of it the same way

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.710
as you think about other
public infrastructure.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:44.550
We passed a trillion dollars to
spend on roads and other things

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.640
in the US.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.370
Open source is a public
infrastructure also.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.020
And like all public
infrastructures,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:53.490
it needs maintenance
and support.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.740
And so we have to figure out
how that's going to work.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.210
It's not at all an easy thing.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.060
But I do think that is part
of the path of the future.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.770
STEPHANIE WONG: Yep, and it's
really hard to predict that.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.670
And so I think having the
secure processes in place from

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.910
build to deploy is
really key, especially

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.180
as we've seen the detrimental
work of adversaries

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.470
coming to light.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.320
Now, can you talk a little
bit about our contributions

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.175
and the work that we've done
in the open source space?

00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:21.300
What are some of the
organizations and the projects

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.858
that we've worked on?

00:04:22.858 --> 00:04:24.525
ERIC BREWER: I'm a
big fan, as you know,

00:04:24.525 --> 00:04:27.330
of kind of raising the level
of attraction over time.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.640
And that gives
developers more leverage.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.400
It allows us to do
more automatically

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.280
on both the security side
and the management side.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.240
And Autopilot is a
great example of that.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.440
It's really-- we will manage the
nodes for you and your cluster,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.970
and you don't have to.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.680
And that is a
security improvement,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.560
but it's also, frankly,
just a toil improvement.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.510
Why do the toil of
managing those nodes

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:49.900
if you don't need to?

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.430
That's probably not what you're
doing your Kubernetes job.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:54.840
You probably have
something you'd

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.810
like to do, like a business app
or website or a data analysis.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.050
Focus on those things, and
we can manage the nodes.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.090
And that general direction
is the way it's going to go.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.843
We can manage more
things over time,

00:05:08.843 --> 00:05:10.260
but they'll be the
things that you

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.770
don't want to manage yourself
because it's a hassle.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:13.770
STEPHANIE WONG: Exactly.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.750
And I do want to dive
a little bit more

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.390
into Google Kubernetes
Engine and what

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.180
we've been doing over
the last couple of years.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.610
I know that we just had GKE's
sixth birthday, actually.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.330
So why don't we go ahead and
save that for the next episode?

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.830
Thank you so much,
once again, Eric.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.230
And to everyone else
watching, stick around

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.910
because we are going
to have more soon.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

